From: alonesniper <433307+alonesniper@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 17:41:56 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] EA SPORTS FC 27 1.8c.cab1: the frame regulator row, its
 global found by walking a running main

The update before 1.8d.310c (1.8c.cab1, main fb81983aa1cc3a2e...) has the same
frame regulator, so the game ran at its own 30 fps there (menus at frame time
p50 33.4 ms) while 1.8d.310c has had its row since the profile was written.

The row could not come from the rewriter's function matching: main has unwind
entries for 405 functions only, and nso_fnmap.py wants both mains on one
machine, so only 1.8d's is here. Instead the global was found at runtime: of
1.54 M pointers dereferenced in main's data and bss, exactly one points to an
object holding the same frame rate twice at +0x8 and +0x10 (30.0, 30.0, with 1
at +0x18), the shape of the known row, at main+0xda19e38, 0x7000 below 1.8d's
global. Writing the target into both took that build's menus 33.4 -> 16.7 ms
and it now holds 60.0 fps in the menus and a Classic Match, and nothing else in
main looked like a frame limit, so this build gets one row.

hle_fpslock.c gets the row. t_fpslock case 22 keys the same checks on the new
build id and the lower global (23 cases). The game note says how a row is
carried across updates now (disassembly where both mains exist, a pointer walk
where only one does), and docs/reference/frame-rate.md records the walk as the
fallback for a game's own frame limit.

Upstream-Status: forwarded seedds/nxnative#2336
Reason: EA SPORTS FC 27 1.8c.cab1 kept its own 30 fps: no frame regulator row for that build
Issue: none (a frame rate row for a game build, no issue)
